cartridge "FUELING STATION/J.KRUTCH/1987"

' FUELING STATION
' A ColecoVision game based on "Fueling Station" by John Krutch, Ahoy!
' December 1987 (Commodore 64 machine language, FUELING.OBJ at $C000,
' started by a two-line BASIC loader, disk ahoy1987-12). Made for Amy
' Studio by Claude (an AI made by Anthropic), 2026, in Amy by Amy Bienvenu.
'
' Not a line-by-line port: the rules are the original's (read from its
' machine code, orig/fueling-obj.dis, and watched in jsc64), the game is
' built for the ColecoVision: a star field, green docking rails with a
' running light, four flying saucers as smooth sprites, a fuel gauge, the
' stick's four directions labelled under the four bays, sound effects.
'
' The game: four saucers bob up and down on their own. Each one flies up
' or down until it reaches the top or the bottom of the sky and turns
' round. Push the stick toward a saucer's label to turn it round at once.
' Bring all four between the docking rails together, and keep them there,
' and they fuel up. Four docks, each one narrower; 150 units of energy
' for each, one used every 40 frames. Out of energy ends the game.
'
' From the original (orig/): the saucers' speeds in pixels a frame, up /
' down: 1/2, 1/1, 2/1, 2/2 (one step of its main loop per video frame,
' waited on the raster); a saucer turns down above Y 60 and up from Y 200
' (C64 sprite rows); the docks are the rows between the two rails, the
' saucer's top row between 113-150, 121-150, 121-142 and 129-142 for docks
' 1-4 (the rails on character rows 8/14, 9/14, 9/13, 10/13); energy 150
' at every dock, one unit every 40 jiffies, with a light running along
' the rails once per unit; a new dock puts saucers 1 and 3 at the bottom
' (Y 198) and 2 and 4 at random heights (60-199); turning a saucer is a
' toggle of its direction (one key each: the C64's four corner keys);
' FUELING IN PROGRESS with a humming sound, then the next dock; after
' dock 4 MISSION ACCOMPLISHED; at energy 0 OUT OF ENERGY.
'
' Liberties: the C64 counted the docking done the very frame all four
' saucers' tops were inside the dock. Left alone, the four saucers'
' different speeds bring them all into the dock by chance: a simulation
' of the original rules wins dock 1 in 44% of games without touching a
' key (6% at dock 4). Here the saucers must stay docked together for a
' moment (60, 40, 30, 16 frames at docks 1-4; a gauge in the status line
' fills up and empties if one leaves), and to make that humanly possible
' a saucer between the rails flies at half speed (the docking field).
' With these two changes no idle game won a dock in 1000 simulated tries,
' Dock 4 is also eased: its top edge is 4 pixels higher (saucer tops
' 125-142, not 129-142; its top rail is drawn 4 pixels higher) and the hold
' is 16 frames, because at 129-142 a bot reacting 15 frames late docked
' it in only 1 of 3 runs (13% in simulation; now about 80%, close to dock
' 3). Also: energy left
' at each dock is added up as a score, with a best score on the title
' (the C64 kept no score); a saucer between the rails lights up; the
' stick replaces the keys (left, up, down, right for saucers 1-4; the
' diagonals turn two at once); the playfield is 24 rows (the C64's 25 rows
' moved down one row; the saucers 16 pixels wide where the C64's were 48);
' the C64 started over at dock 1 after OUT OF ENERGY or MISSION
' ACCOMPLISHED (PRESS ANY KEY TO PLAY AGAIN): here the game ends with the
' message and goes back to the title; a title page, a credits page and a
' title tune.
'
' Controls: controller 1 (or 2): stick left, up, down, right turns saucer
' 1, 2, 3, 4 round. On the title, up and down choose, a button chooses.
'
' House rules: text in columns 2-29, rows 1-22; tiles from code 128; the
' frame loop is protected from the NMI; screens drawn with the NMI off
' after a wait, display off. Music first in the sound table, effects
' after; the title tune is played once. `set sprite count` is always
' given. Own xorshift random numbers.

